  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Integrated SGBV Response and Preventions Services for Women and Gi</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Much has changed in South Africa since 1994, including a number of new laws to protect victims of violence. However, these laws are poorly enforced and levels of violence remain high. In South Africa, it is estimated that a rape happens every eight seconds and that one in every two women will be raped in their life. Yet only one in 36 rape cases will be reported, of which only 15% result in successful convictions. Masisikumeni is the only non-state provider of sexual and gender based violence (SGBV) services in Nkomazi in Mpumalanga province, a predominantly rural farming area. In Nkomazi, traditional beliefs and negative cultural attitudes about the roles of and relationships between women and men sustain unequal power relations. Violence and abuse are frequent experiences for women and girls. In August 2012 alone, 46 rape cases were reported at one of the police stations Masisikumeni works with. Even after reporting to police stations, women and girls struggle to access quality support and services from the police, health and judicial services. The changes to be bought about through this grant include: increased confidence and empowerment of survivors through counselling, medical and legal support; improved cultural attitudes and practices; and a justice system that is more responsive to survivors. Finally, Masisikumeni&apos;s own capacity and systems will be strengthened so it can become an even more effective organisation which successfully reduces the prevalence and impact of SGBV in Mpumalanga and is recognised as a leader in its field.</narrative>
  </description>
